### Why start simple before ML?

Simple baselines deliver most of the value cheaply, need no training, are easy to reason about, and give a baseline to beat. You only add ML when simple methods plateau.

```java
package com.bank.recommend.simple;

import java.util.List;

public class SimpleRecommender {

    public List<String> trendingProducts() {
        // Most engaged with in the last 7 days
        return List.of("Home Loan", "Fixed Deposit", "Credit Card");
    }

    public List<String> categoryRules(String currentProduct) {
        // If customer has a savings account, suggest FD
        if ("Savings Account".equals(currentProduct)) {
            return List.of("Fixed Deposit", "Recurring Deposit");
        }
        return trendingProducts();
    }
}
```

### How does collaborative filtering work?

Recommend based on behavior patterns across users. At scale, factorize the sparse interaction matrix into latent vectors.

```java
package com.bank.recommend.collaborative;

import java.util.*;

public class SimpleCollaborativeFilter {

    // User-item interaction matrix (simplified)
    private final Map<String, Set<String>> userProducts = Map.of(
        "Rajesh",  Set.of("Savings", "FD"),
        "Priya",   Set.of("Savings", "FD", "Home Loan"),
        "Pankaj",  Set.of("Savings", "Credit Card")
    );

    public List<String> recommend(String userId) {
        Set<String> myProducts = userProducts.getOrDefault(userId, Set.of());
        Map<String, Integer> scores = new HashMap<>();

        for (var entry : userProducts.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getKey().equals(userId)) continue;
            // Find similar users (overlap in products)
            long overlap = entry.getValue().stream()
                .filter(myProducts::contains).count();
            if (overlap > 0) {
                // Recommend products they have that I do not
                entry.getValue().stream()
                    .filter(p -> !myProducts.contains(p))
                    .forEach(p -> scores.merge(p, (int) overlap, Integer::sum));
            }
        }

        return scores.entrySet().stream()
            .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Integer>comparingByValue().reversed())
            .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
            .toList();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SimpleCollaborativeFilter cf = new SimpleCollaborativeFilter();
        System.out.println("Recommendations for Rajesh: " + cf.recommend("Rajesh"));
    }
}
```

```text
Recommendations for Rajesh: [Home Loan, Credit Card]
# Priya has Savings+FD like Rajesh, and also has Home Loan -> recommend it
# Pankaj has Savings like Rajesh, and also has Credit Card -> recommend it
```

### How does content-based filtering work?

Recommend items whose attributes are similar to items the user already liked.

```java
package com.bank.recommend.contentbased;

public class ContentBasedFilter {

    // Each product has attributes: category, risk, tenure
    // Rajesh likes FD (savings, low-risk, medium-tenure)
    // -> Recommend: Recurring Deposit (savings, low-risk, short-tenure)
    // -> Do NOT recommend: Equity Fund (investment, high-risk)

    public String explainRecommendation(String userId, String product) {
        return "Recommended because you hold a Fixed Deposit " 
            + "and " + product + " has similar savings attributes.";
    }
}
```

### How does offline precomputation and real-time ranking work?

Heavy candidate generation runs in batch. A lightweight ranker re-orders with fresh context at request time.

```java
package com.bank.recommend.pipeline;

import java.util.List;

public class TwoStageRecommender {

    private final OfflineCandidateStore candidateStore;
    private final RealTimeRanker ranker;

    public TwoStageRecommender(OfflineCandidateStore candidateStore,
                                RealTimeRanker ranker) {
        this.candidateStore = candidateStore;
        this.ranker = ranker;
    }

    public List<String> recommend(String userId) {
        // Stage 1: Load precomputed candidates (top 200)
        List<String> candidates = candidateStore.getCandidates(userId);

        // Stage 2: Re-rank with fresh context
        return ranker.rank(userId, candidates);
        // Uses: time of day, current session, last viewed product
        // Returns: top 10, final order
    }
}
```

