### How do you handle cold-start?

New users see popular items plus onboarding signals. New items use content attributes plus exploratory exposure.

```java
package com.bank.recommend.coldstart;

import java.util.List;

public class ColdStartHandler {

    public List<String> forNewUser(String demographic) {
        // No history -> show popular + demographic-based
        if ("salaried-25-35".equals(demographic)) {
            return List.of("Credit Card", "Home Loan", "Savings Account");
        }
        return List.of("Fixed Deposit", "Savings Account"); // safe default
    }

    public double forNewItem(String productId) {
        // No interactions -> use content attributes to place near similar items
        // Give exploratory exposure (show to some users regardless of score)
        return 0.1; // exploration boost
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Jumping to deep learning on day one without a simple baseline. Not having an A/B testing framework. Creating filter bubbles by never showing diverse items.

```java
package com.bank.recommend.antipattern;

// WRONG: complex ML model on day one with no baseline
// No way to know if it is better than just showing popular items.
// Start simple, measure, then add ML when simple plateaus.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How do you handle the filter bubble problem? How do you ensure compliance (not recommending products the user is not eligible for)? How would you implement real-time model serving? What is the difference between implicit and explicit feedback? How do you handle seasonality in recommendations?
